 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "dyson keeps pulsin on n off like its stutterin, wont stay on proper. batt is full. pls help"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Switch it off and unplug it (or take the battery off) first. That stuttering is usually the vacuum protecting itself because airflow is blocked or a filter needs washing. Since the battery is full, let's check for blockages and filters first. 1. Empty the bin completely.\n2. Remove the pre-motor filter (usually at the top) and wash it in cold water with no soap. Let it dry fully for 24 hours before putting it back – a damp filter causes pulsing.\n3. Check the wand and floorhead for blockages (like coins or hair). While you do that, could you tell me the model number? It’s usually on a sticker on the body of the vacuum.\n[Helpful to see: image \"Poor suction — what to check first\" — A vacuum that pulses or cuts out after running is usually a full bin, a dirty filter or a blockage starving the airflow — check and clear those before suspecting battery, charger or motor. Switch off and unplug first. Typical layout — your model may differ.]\n[Panel: \"To check a suitable replacement — Enter your model number below. Once we have the model I can check whether a suitable replacement is available. It's usually on a sticker inside the door or around the frame, or on the back of the appliance.\"]"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "its a v8 animal, sv10 on the label. emptied the bin, was full of dog hair, still pulsin. no burning smell or heat tho"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Please detach the long tube and the floorhead from the main body. Look through each one to see if there is anything stuck inside, like a coin or hair wad. Also check the inlet where the wand connects to the vacuum body. Did you find any blockages, or was everything clear?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "yeah big clump of hair and cat litter stuck at the bottom of the wand where the head goes on, pulled it out. its fine now, proper suction, no pulsin"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That’s excellent news. The pulsing was the vacuum’s protection system kicking in because that hair and litter blockage stopped the airflow; clearing it has fixed the issue. To help prevent this from happening again with pet hair, try to empty the bin more frequently and give the wand a quick once-over if you notice suction dropping. Is there anything else I can help you with today?"
  }
 ],
